1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,525
How Opium Paid for Colonial Singapore

2
00:00:03,525 --> 00:00:07,155
For most of the nineteenth
century, the government that

3
00:00:07,235 --> 00:00:10,675
ran Singapore paid its
bills with money from opium.

4
00:00:10,675 --> 00:00:14,268
Not through smuggling, and
not by looking the other way,

5
00:00:14,348 --> 00:00:18,007
but openly: the colonial
administration auctioned off the

6
00:00:18,087 --> 00:00:22,402
exclusive right to prepare and sell
the drug to the highest bidder,

7
00:00:22,482 --> 00:00:25,419
and for decades the
proceeds covered somewhere

8
00:00:25,499 --> 00:00:29,500
between 40 and 60 percent of
everything the government spent.

9
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:33,637
It was the single largest source
of public revenue in a free

10
00:00:33,717 --> 00:00:36,729
port that famously
charged no import duties,

11
00:00:36,809 --> 00:00:41,719
and the arrangement ran for well over
a century before Singapore turned

12
00:00:41,799 --> 00:00:45,875
around and made the very trade
that had funded it a crime.

13
00:00:45,875 --> 00:00:50,003
The system was called the revenue
farm, and it began in 1820,

14
00:00:50,083 --> 00:00:53,038
a year after Raffles
founded the settlement,

15
00:00:53,118 --> 00:00:57,107
when Resident William Farquhar
introduced it as a quick way

16
00:00:57,187 --> 00:01:00,832
to raise money without
imposing the kind of taxes that

17
00:01:00,912 --> 00:01:04,775
would have undercut Singapore's
standing as a free port.

18
00:01:04,775 --> 00:01:07,317
Rather than run the opium trade itself,

19
00:01:07,397 --> 00:01:12,493
the government auctioned off a monopoly
licence: whoever bid the highest sum,

20
00:01:12,573 --> 00:01:16,122
payable monthly in advance,
won the exclusive right to

21
00:01:16,202 --> 00:01:19,618
import raw opium, boil it
down into smokable chandu,

22
00:01:19,698 --> 00:01:24,000
and sell it retail through a
network of licensed shops and dens.

23
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,181
Everything the farmer
collected above what he had

24
00:01:27,261 --> 00:01:30,442
bid was his to keep, and
the government never had

25
00:01:30,522 --> 00:01:33,650
to touch the drug itself,
only bank the cheque.

26
00:01:33,650 --> 00:01:38,433
The same auction system covered
spirits, gambling and pawnbroking,

27
00:01:38,513 --> 00:01:42,050
but opium was by far the
richest prize on offer.

28
00:01:42,050 --> 00:01:46,320
By the 1870s, the biggest of
these prizes had consolidated

29
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:50,370
into the hands of a small
group of Chinese businessmen

30
00:01:50,450 --> 00:01:54,275
known to colonial officials
as the Great Syndicate.

31
00:01:54,275 --> 00:01:57,193
Its leading figure was Cheang Hong Lim,

32
00:01:57,273 --> 00:02:01,500
the eldest son of an already
prominent merchant family.

33
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:05,806
From 1871 to 1879, Cheang
and his partners Tan Seng Poh

34
00:02:05,886 --> 00:02:09,953
and Tan Hiok Nee held not
just Singapore's opium and

35
00:02:10,033 --> 00:02:14,260
spirit farms but Johor's,
Melaka's and Riau's as well,

36
00:02:14,340 --> 00:02:19,125
running what amounted to a
private cartel across the strait.

37
00:02:19,125 --> 00:02:22,337
Cheang was also, by the
standards of the age,

38
00:02:22,417 --> 00:02:26,873
a generous public benefactor:
in 1876 he funded the conversion

39
00:02:26,953 --> 00:02:31,335
of open ground in front of the
police office into Singapore's

40
00:02:31,415 --> 00:02:34,773
first public garden,
which was renamed Hong Lim

41
00:02:34,853 --> 00:02:38,950
Green in his honour and
survives today as Hong Lim Park.

42
00:02:38,950 --> 00:02:43,398
The syndicate's local farms sat
on top of a much longer supply

43
00:02:43,478 --> 00:02:48,225
chain that made Singapore's position
possible in the first place.

44
00:02:48,225 --> 00:02:52,180
Raw opium arrived from British
India in two main grades:

45
00:02:52,260 --> 00:02:56,360
Bengal opium, grown around
Patna and Benares and auctioned

46
00:02:56,440 --> 00:02:59,746
at Calcutta under a strict
government monopoly,

47
00:02:59,826 --> 00:03:04,150
and Malwa opium, grown further
west and auctioned at Bombay.

48
00:03:04,150 --> 00:03:07,421
With no tariffs of its
own and a location roughly

49
00:03:07,501 --> 00:03:10,841
between both auction cities
and the Chinese coast,

50
00:03:10,921 --> 00:03:14,807
Singapore became the key
transshipment point where the two

51
00:03:14,887 --> 00:03:18,375
supplies merged before
moving on to Chinese buyers.

52
00:03:18,375 --> 00:03:22,329
The trade could run at
real scale: in one week

53
00:03:22,409 --> 00:03:25,836
in January 1836, four ships alone landed

54
00:03:25,916 --> 00:03:29,950
almost 3,900 chests of
opium through the port.

55
00:03:29,950 --> 00:03:32,882
All of this made opium a fiscal engine

56
00:03:32,962 --> 00:03:36,450
unlike anything else
in the colony's budget.

57
00:03:36,450 --> 00:03:39,980
Across the nineteenth century
and into the twentieth,

58
00:03:40,060 --> 00:03:44,271
opium revenue is estimated to
have covered somewhere between 40

59
00:03:44,351 --> 00:03:48,971
and 60 percent of the Straits
Settlements government's entire income,

60
00:03:49,051 --> 00:03:52,525
not a niche source of funds
but the backbone of it.

61
00:03:52,525 --> 00:03:56,969
The dependence went beyond ordinary
domestic spending: in 1914,

62
00:03:57,049 --> 00:04:02,212
the Straits Settlements contributed
the largest share of military funding

63
00:04:02,292 --> 00:04:06,234
of any Crown Colony to the
Imperial Exchequer in London,

64
00:04:06,314 --> 00:04:11,700
and more than half of that contribution
was drawn from opium revenue alone.

65
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:15,910
A free port that prided itself
on charging no duties was,

66
00:04:15,990 --> 00:04:18,850
in practice, running on a single drug.

67
00:04:18,850 --> 00:04:23,000
That revenue was collected
one pipe at a time.

68
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:27,488
Licensed opium shops and dens
lined South Bridge Road, Boat Quay,

69
00:04:27,568 --> 00:04:31,775
Amoy Street, Carpenter Street,
Bugis Street and Malay Street,

70
00:04:31,855 --> 00:04:36,775
doing business openly under whichever
syndicate held the current farm.

71
00:04:36,775 --> 00:04:40,592
The map below marks where the
story played out on the ground:

72
00:04:40,672 --> 00:04:45,576
not just that den row through Chinatown,
but Hong Lim Park a few streets over,

73
00:04:45,656 --> 00:04:48,770
and Havelock Road, where
the Chinese Protectorate,

74
00:04:48,850 --> 00:04:52,731
the colonial body that oversaw
much of Chinese community life,

75
00:04:52,811 --> 00:04:58,098
from newly arrived labourers to secret
societies, moved into a new building in 1886,

76
00:04:58,178 --> 00:05:01,500
among the same population
most exposed to the trade.

77
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:04,440
Organised opposition to the trade had been

78
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:07,900
building for years before
the government acted.

79
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:12,677
Dr Lim Boon Keng, a physician
and Legislative Council member,

80
00:05:12,757 --> 00:05:18,569
co-founded an Anti-Opium Society in
1906, the same year his associate S.C.

81
00:05:18,649 --> 00:05:23,825
Yin opened a refuge in Singapore
to help addicts break the habit.

82
00:05:23,825 --> 00:05:28,910
The Singapore Anti-Opium Society
was formally inaugurated in 1907,

83
00:05:28,990 --> 00:05:32,119
the same year a colonial Opium Commission

84
00:05:32,199 --> 00:05:36,425
was appointed to investigate
the scale of the problem.

85
00:05:36,425 --> 00:05:40,645
Its 1909 findings led directly
to the Chandu Revenue Ordinance,

86
00:05:40,725 --> 00:05:43,989
which abolished the private
farm system outright:

87
00:05:44,069 --> 00:05:48,425
from then on, the government would
prepare and sell opium itself,

88
00:05:48,505 --> 00:05:52,383
through a Monopolies Department
formed the following year,

89
00:05:52,463 --> 00:05:55,125
rather than auction the privilege away.

90
00:05:55,125 --> 00:05:57,937
Ending the private farm did not end the

91
00:05:58,017 --> 00:06:01,650
government's dependence
on opium money overnight.

92
00:06:01,650 --> 00:06:05,147
It took until 1925 for the
colony to formally commit to

93
00:06:05,227 --> 00:06:09,309
weaning itself off the revenue,
when it set up the Opium Revenue

94
00:06:09,389 --> 00:06:12,951
Replacement Reserve Fund to
absorb the coming shortfall,

95
00:06:13,031 --> 00:06:16,594
and 1930 before a purpose-built
factory at Pasir Panjang

96
00:06:16,674 --> 00:06:19,195
began sealing measured doses into small,

97
00:06:19,275 --> 00:06:22,967
tamper-evident tins, stamped
with date and place of issue,

98
00:06:23,047 --> 00:06:27,925
tightening the government's own grip
on a retail trade it now ran directly.

99
00:06:27,925 --> 00:06:31,170
What the trade cost the
people who actually used it

100
00:06:31,250 --> 00:06:34,886
is harder to pin down than
what it earned the government,

101
00:06:34,966 --> 00:06:37,900
which mostly counted
money rather than lives.

102
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:41,883
The 1907 Opium Commission
itself found that the worst of

103
00:06:41,963 --> 00:06:46,309
the harm fell on the poor, who
were often reduced to scraping

104
00:06:46,389 --> 00:06:50,525
and re-smoking the dregs of
opium already used by others.

105
00:06:50,525 --> 00:06:54,656
Anti-opium campaigners like
Lim Boon Keng had been making

106
00:06:54,736 --> 00:06:58,800
that same argument for years
before the Commission sat.

107
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:02,400
It took a world war to finally end things.

108
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:06,386
Opium smoking was banned
outright under the Japanese

109
00:07:06,466 --> 00:07:11,313
Occupation in November 1943, and
when the British returned they

110
00:07:11,393 --> 00:07:15,850
kept the wartime ban rather
than reopen the old monopoly.

111
00:07:15,850 --> 00:07:19,870
By 1951, the Dangerous Drugs
Ordinance had turned what

112
00:07:19,950 --> 00:07:24,198
was once a major line item in
the government's own budget

113
00:07:24,278 --> 00:07:28,150
into a criminal offence
punishable by imprisonment.

114
00:07:28,150 --> 00:07:31,475
The physician and social
reformer Chen Su Lan,

115
00:07:31,555 --> 00:07:35,397
who was elected president
of the Singapore Anti-Opium

116
00:07:35,477 --> 00:07:40,653
Society in 1930 and campaigned against
the trade for decades afterward,

117
00:07:40,733 --> 00:07:43,175
lived to see it finally outlawed.

118
00:07:43,175 --> 00:07:47,700
Cheang Hong Lim himself did
not live to see any of this.

119
00:07:47,700 --> 00:07:51,503
He died in 1893, sixteen
years before the farm

120
00:07:51,583 --> 00:07:55,550
system that had made his
fortune was abolished.

121
00:07:55,550 --> 00:07:58,725
His tomb still stands
today, and Hong Lim Park,

122
00:07:58,805 --> 00:08:03,573
the garden he funded, remains one
of the few places in Singapore where

123
00:08:03,653 --> 00:08:07,451
the name of a nineteenth-century
opium farmer is written

124
00:08:07,531 --> 00:08:11,825
plainly into the landscape rather
than quietly left out of it.

125
00:08:11,825 --> 00:08:14,138
Where it fits in the bigger story:

126
00:08:14,218 --> 00:08:18,079
Singapore's free port famously
charged no import duties,

127
00:08:18,159 --> 00:08:22,100
and for most of its colonial
history it did not need to.

128
00:08:22,100 --> 00:08:24,575
It had opium instead.

129
00:08:24,575 --> 00:08:28,986
The drug that other colonial
governments fought to keep out of their

130
00:08:29,066 --> 00:08:33,411
own populations was, for Singapore,
the single most reliable source

131
00:08:33,491 --> 00:08:37,836
of government income there was,
until public pressure and a foreign

132
00:08:37,916 --> 00:08:42,275
occupation did what decades of
reform commissions alone could not.
